{"op": "append", "content": "[\u26a0\ufe0f] [2026-01-20 05:22:12 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-SessionContext] [Project: session_context_research] Found session_utils.py provides canonical session key derivation: get_canonical_session_key() returns stable_session_id (preferred) with fallback to session_id. This module was created to fix session isolation bugs by providing single source of truth. However, set_project.py and logging_utils.py are NOT using this function - they have inline derivation logic! | confidence=0.9; entry_type=finding; file=shared/session_utils.py; gap_identified=True; lines=1-80; reasoning={\"how\": \"Code inspection of session_utils.py and comparison with set_project/logging_utils usage patterns\", \"what\": \"session_utils.get_canonical_session_key() exists as single source of truth, but set_project and logging_utils have their own inline derivation logic (not using this function), creating potential inconsistency\", \"why\": \"Need to understand canonical session key derivation and whether it's consistently used\"}; stage=research; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/session_context_research/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-20T05:22:12.583799+00:00_d805756c", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T05:22:12.583839+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-20T05:22:12.583799+00:00_d805756c", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T05:22:12.594896+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-20 05:22:37 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-SessionContext] [Project: session_context_research] Traced agent_sessions table operations: get_or_create_agent_session(identity_key) does INSERT OR IGNORE with new UUID, then SELECT session_id WHERE identity_key. This means same identity_key always returns same session_id (deterministic). The identity_key is SHA-256 hash of (repo_root, mode, scope_key, agent_key) from server.py derive_session_identity_preview(). | confidence=0.95; entry_type=finding; file=storage/sqlite.py; lines=2103-2162; reasoning={\"how\": \"Code inspection of SQLite storage backend agent_sessions table operations\", \"what\": \"get_or_create_agent_session uses upsert pattern - INSERT OR IGNORE with new UUID, then SELECT by identity_key returns same session_id for same identity, making it deterministic\", \"why\": \"Need to understand how stable_session_id is created and stored in agent_sessions table\"}; stage=research; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/session_context_research/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-20T05:22:37.340358+00:00_425fe09b", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T05:22:37.340446+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-20T05:22:37.340358+00:00_425fe09b", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T05:22:37.351310+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u274c] [2026-01-20 05:22:51 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-SessionContext] [Project: session_context_research] CRITICAL BUG IDENTIFIED: In project mode, scope_key = execution_id (UUID, changes every request). In derive_session_identity() line 300, each append_entry call gets NEW execution_id, so identity_key hash is DIFFERENT each time. This means each append_entry creates a NEW session in agent_sessions table instead of reusing the session from set_project! | confidence=0.98; entry_type=bug_discovery; file=server.py; lines=287-314; reasoning={\"how\": \"Code inspection of derive_session_identity() - execution_id is regenerated for each MCP tool call, breaking session stability\", \"what\": \"Root cause found - scope_key in project mode uses execution_id (fresh UUID per request), so identity hash changes every request, creating new sessions instead of reusing existing binding\", \"why\": \"Need to understand why stable_session_id is different between set_project and append_entry\"}; severity=critical; stage=research; priority=high; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/session_context_research/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-20T05:22:51.841987+00:00_c0fd4221", "timestamp": "2026-01-20T05:22:51.842026+00:00"}
